### Sampling: Determines where we measure the image.
### Quantization: Determines what numerical value we assign to the measurement.
Therefore:Digital image = spatial sampling + numerical quantization
# 9. WHY DO CHANNELS MATTER?
Channels determine what information is available.
# 10. COLOR SPACES
A color space is a way of representing colors numerically.Mainly useful for displaying/representing color.
# 12. DATA TYPE
Pixels are numbers, but the data type determines how those numbers are stored and processed.
uint8/int16/float32/float64
Useful for mathematical processing and ML.
# 13. NORMALIZATION
An image with:0 – 255 can be converted to:0 – 1.Normalization makes numerical processing easier and is commonly used before ML/deep-learning models.Changing data type does not necessarily change the visual meaning.It changes **how the numerical values are represented and processed**.
#MIN AND MAX
Image A:min = 0.max = 255
→ wide intensity range
Image B:min = 100.max = 150
→ narrow intensity range
#RESOLUTION: Resolution describes the spatial dimensions/detail capability of an image or imaging system.
Example:1920 × 1080 means:
Width  = 1920 pixels.Height = 1080 pixels.Total pixels:1920 × 1080 = 2,073,600 pixels
HIGHER RESOLUTION: MORE MEMORY MORE COMPUTATION MORE DETAIL MORE SPATIAL SAMPLES
LOWER RESOLUTION: FEWER DETAIL FEWER COMPUTATION FEWER MEMORY FASTER PROCESSING.
#VIDEO AND FRAMES
A video is a sequence of frames:30 FPS=approximately 30 frames/second
Comparing consecutive frames can help detect movement.
Frame 1:Car at position A. Frame 2:Car at position B
Calculate: Difference = |Frame2 - Frame1|.Changed regions can indicate motion.
